  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHeat and humidity will continue to build as the day progresses,\nwith highs in the mid to upper 80s and dewpoints back in the \nupper 60s to low 70s. Rain and thunderstorms have moved into the\narea from the west and south and will continue to spread across\nthe region as the afternoon progresses. CAPE has really\nincreased over the past hour, with areas across NEPA into the\nSouthern Tier already seeing values above 2000 j/kg with most of\nthe CIN mixed out. To go along with the increased\ninstability, effective bulk shear of 30kts has spread across the\nregion ahead of the approaching shortwave. This will support\nloosely organized clusters of thunderstorms with main threats \nisolated damaging winds and heavy rain causing instances of \nisolated flash flooding. Best chances for severe weather\ncurrently look to be cross the Wyoming Valley into the Catskills\nwhere higher CAPE and shear values could produce some better \ncluster organization. Storms should stick around into the\nevening hours.\n\nHeavy rain will be accompany some of these storms, allowing for a \nchance for isolated flash flooding across areas that have been \nsaturated from the recent rains. PWATs remain under 2in but with\nthe early storms and expected rain and storms later this\nafternoon, flash flooding risks will continue. \n\nOn Saturday, another weak shortwave will ripple through the area, \nkicking off another round of showers and storms in the afternoon and \nevening. The severe threat looks very low as bulk shear is forecast \nto be weak and CAPE a lower than today. Heavy rain chances will\nremain, with isolated flash flooding possible across areas \nsaturated by recent rains. A Marginal Risk for flash flooding \ncontinues across the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\n***The forecast for Sunday into next week has not changed much\n since the last forecast package, thus, the discussion remains \n unchanged.***\n\nA welcome reprieve from heat and rain is expected on Sunday as weak \nhigh pressure builds into the area. Temps in the upper 70s to mid \n80s are expected, but the much lower humidity will make it feel \nquite nice outside. \n\nA weather pattern change starting at the beginning of next week will \nbring continued shower chances through the week, but temperatures \nand humidity will be trending lower. The Bermuda high is expected to \nweaken and slide south over the Bahamas into the Gulf as more zonal \nflow develops across the Great Lakes into the Northeast. This will \nend the southerly flow pushing the higher humidity into the area and \nallow for Canadian air to filter into the region, bringing cooler \ntemperatures through the week. A few shortwaves will ripple through \nthe pattern, kicking off scattered showers through mid week. A \nstronger shortwave with a cold front is progged to move through the \narea sometime during the end of the week, bringing high pressure, \ndry conditions and cooler temps for the weekend.",
